# 5. THE FAIRNESS DOCTRINE

**This is the most important section in this prompt.** Read it twice.

## 5.1 Core Fairness Principle

> **A bot should feel like a *skilled human opponent who is trying their best* — not a *perfect machine that is exploiting you*.**

Every AI decision must be evaluated against this principle. If a bot behavior feels "unfair," it must be removed or downgraded — **even if it makes the bot "smarter."**

## 5.2 The Hidden Information Rule

Bots may only use information that a human player could observe:

| Allowed | Disallowed |
|---|---|
| Enemy's current position (if visible) | Enemy's position through walls |
| Enemy's HP bar (visible overhead) | Enemy's exact HP value |
| Enemy's last-seen heading | Enemy's hidden mana value |
| Enemy projectiles in flight | Enemy's cooldown timers |
| Storm zone edge | Exact storm shrink time |
| Loot visible on screen | Loot hidden under fog |

**Implementation**: Bots have a `perception` object with fields:
- `visibleEnemies`: array of enemies with line-of-sight
- `lastKnownPositions`: map of enemyId → {x, y, timeLastSeen}
- `heardSounds`: recent sound events (weapon fires, dashes) with direction and time
- `reactionQueue`: pending "I need to react to X" events with delayed timers

## 5.3 The Reaction Time Floor

Every bot has a `reactionTime` value in milliseconds. Before a bot can "respond" to a new stimulus (enemy appears, projectile inbound, player enters attack range), it must wait `reactionTime` ms.

**Reaction time by tier:**

| Tier | Reaction Time | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| NOOB | 380-450ms | Very slow, misses obvious threats |
| ROOKIE | 320-380ms | Still slow, feels human-beginner |
| AVERAGE | 260-320ms | Human-casual equivalent |
| SKILLED | 200-260ms | Human-intermediate equivalent |
| VETERAN | 160-200ms | Human-advanced equivalent |
| ELITE | 130-160ms | Human-expert equivalent |
| PRO | 110-130ms | Human-pro equivalent |

**No bot ever reacts faster than 110ms.**

## 5.4 The Damage Cap Rule

When the player is being focused by bots, damage must be throttled:

```
If (player took damage from bots in last 2s > player.maxHp * 0.55):
    All bot damage to player reduced by 30% for next 1s
    Player's next dodge grants 2x invulnerability duration
    All bots within 200px of player apply a 0.3s "hesitation" delay before their next attack
```

This ensures the player **cannot be instant-killed** by a swarm of bots.

## 5.5 The Grace Period Rule

When a player is at ≤ 15% HP:

- Bots deal 25% reduced damage to the player for 3 seconds
- Bots lose "aim lead" precision (increased aim error)
- Bots within 150px apply a "backing off" behavior once per 8 seconds (they take one step back before re-engaging)
- If player uses a dash while at ≤ 15% HP, the dash is 40% longer

This gives the player a chance to **make a comeback** instead of dying instantly.

## 5.6 The "Showmanship" Rule

Bots must occasionally make "believable mistakes" to seem human. This is NOT a bug — it is a **required feature**:

- Every 15-25 seconds (random per bot), a bot makes **one** of the following:
  - Misses an easy shot (increased aim error on one shot)
  - Dashes in a suboptimal direction (random direction instead of optimal)
  - Hesitates for 0.3s before initiating an attack
  - Walks into a wall briefly (then corrects)
  - Fires at a target that's about to die anyway (secures a kill, but wastes mana)

**Rate this by skill tier:**
- NOOB: 1 showmanship per 8s
- AVERAGE: 1 per 15s
- PRO: 1 per 30s (they make "rare" mistakes)

## 5.7 The Rubber-Band Rule

Difficulty is **dynamically adjusted in real-time** based on player performance (see §6). Bots get easier when the player is struggling, harder when the player is winning. The band width is capped:

- **Max buff**: +2 skill tiers above the player's MMR-derived tier
- **Max debuff**: -2 skill tiers below the player's MMR-derived tier
- **Never below NOOB** and **never above PRO**

This prevents bots from feeling either "impossibly hard" or "tragically dumb."

## 5.8 The Player Agency Rule

The player must always feel in control. Bots must **never**:
- Trap the player in an unavoidable damage sequence
- Kill the player without the player having had at least 2 "escape chances" (dash, medkit, cover)
- Deal damage to the player from a location the player could not reach or see

If a bot is about to kill the player, and the player has **never** been given an escape opportunity in the last 2 seconds, the bot must **hesitate** (skip its next attack) to give the player 0.5s.

