# 15. FAIRNESS LAYER (ANTI-FRUSTRATION SYSTEMS)

This layer **overrides** the AI. Every frame, it runs after the AI decides an action.

## 15.1 Damage Throttle

```javascript
// In takeDamage, after damage is computed:
if (target.isPlayer) {
    const timeSinceFirstHitInBurst = game.time - lastDamageStartTime;
    const damageInBurst = damageAccumulator;
    
    if (timeSinceFirstHitInBurst < 2.0 && damageAccumulator > target.maxHp * 0.55) {
        // Player is being bursted - reduce damage
        damage *= 0.7;
    }
    
    // Grace period
    if (target.hp < target.maxHp * 0.15) {
        damage *= 0.75;  // 25% reduction at low HP
    }
    
    // Anti-focus
    if (botsCurrentlyTargetingPlayer >= 3) {
        damage *= 0.8;
    }
}
```

## 15.2 Reaction Time Floor Enforcement

Every bot's action has a `timeSinceStimulus` value. If `timeSinceStimulus < bot.reactionTime`, the action is **deferred**:

```javascript
// In handleStimulus:
if (game.time - stimulus.detectedAt < bot.reactionTime) {
    // Not ready to act yet
    return;
}
```

## 15.3 Aim Error Injection

Every bot's aim has error based on:
- Base tier error
- Distance to target
- Recent player dodges (if player dodged 3 times, error increases 20%)
- Rubber-band state

```javascript
function applyAimError(bot, target, baseAngle) {
    let errorRad = bot.aimError;
    
    // Distance scaling (error is larger at distance)
    const dist = distance(bot, target);
    errorRad *= (1 + dist / 500);
    
    // Rubber-band adjustment
    if (playerMomentum < -0.3) errorRad *= 1.3;  // player losing - bots miss more
    if (playerMomentum > +0.5) errorRad *= 0.9;  // player winning - bots miss less
    
    // Random scatter
    const angleError = (Math.random() - 0.5) * errorRad * 2;
    return baseAngle + angleError;
}
```

## 15.4 The "Fair Bot" Pause

When a bot is about to **kill the player**, and the player has used **no** defensive cooldowns in the last 1.5s:

```javascript
if (bot.willKillPlayer && player.lastDefensiveCooldownUsed > 1.5) {
    // Bot "shows mercy" - pauses briefly, giving player a chance
    bot.hesitationTimer = 0.5;
    return;
}
```

This gives the player a chance to dodge/medkit.

## 15.5 "Fair Loss" Behavior

When player HP < 10%:

- Bots within 100px **do not attack** for 0.8s (they "wait for the player to react")
- After 0.8s, if player hasn't escaped, bots resume attack
- This gives the player a "clutch moment"

## 15.6 Cooldown Mirror

Bots **cannot** use abilities during cooldown (already true), but additionally:

- Bots respect a **minimum cooldown** longer than the player's cooldown by 10%
- This creates a **subtle** difficulty offset where the player's cooldowns feel "faster"

## 15.7 The "No Doom Stack" Rule

A bot will not:
- Attack a player at < 15% HP if the bot is already at full HP and the player has no allies (too easy a kill — the bot "gives" the player a break by wandering off)

Wait — this rule is controversial. Let me rephrase:

- A bot will not attack a player at < 15% HP if the bot is **PRO-tier** and the player is **novice-tier MMR** (the bot "knows" it doesn't need to rush)

Better: bots **reduce aggression** when the player is nearly dead AND the player's MMR is much lower than the bot's tier.

## 15.8 The Rubber-Band Aggression Curve

```
Player HP | Bot Aggression Multiplier
100%      | 1.0x
80%       | 1.0x
60%       | 1.05x
40%       | 1.1x
20%       | 0.85x (going easy)
10%       | 0.7x
<10%      | 0.5x (mercy mode)
```

Bots attack more aggressively when the player is healthy, less when the player is dying.

## 15.9 Rubber-Band on Bots (Catch-Up)

If player HP > 80% AND has 0 deaths:

- Bots are **more accurate** (this gives player a challenge)
- Bots coordinate more (to create engaging fights)
- But still bounded by fairness floors

